    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Line (3) is wrong: $f(x+h)=(x+h)^2$, not $x^2+h$.",
      "worked": "Line (3) substitutes into only part of the function. The correct substitution is $f(x+h)=(x+h)^2=x^2+2xh+h^2$. Hence \n\n$$\\begin{align*}f'(x)&=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{(x+h)^2-x^2}{h}\\\\f'(x)&=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{2xh+h^2}{h}\\\\f'(x)&=\\lim_{h\\to0}(2x+h)\\\\f'(x)&=2x.\\end{align*}$$\n\n",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
